From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.3-SAGA -- fixes from second ChatGPT review
 round (verified against source; only real items applied)

Applies ON TOP of adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch. Same base as
v3.3.2-SAGA. User ran a second ChatGPT review pass against v3.3.2 and
asked for each of its 17 points to be checked against real code before
applying anything -- not accepted on faith. 6 confirmed real and fixed
here; the rest were judged already-resolved, advisory/non-actionable,
or not worth the added risk given no contradicting evidence. See notes
below for the full breakdown.

CONFIRMED AND FIXED:

1. Residual race in adapt_depth_controller() (the FIRST review round's
   fix, cmpxchg-guarded evaluate+reset, was only partial): the
   atomic_inc(window_good/bad) happens BEFORE the cmpxchg guard, so a
   writer on another CPU could still land a sample in window_good/bad
   in the instant between another CPU's atomic_xchg-reset of those
   counters and its atomic_set of window_total -- corrupting one
   window's count without double-applying an adjustment (the narrower
   bug the first fix left behind). Replaced with a double-buffered
   design: window_good/bad/total each became [2][ADIOS_OPTYPES], with
   a window_buf_idx[ADIOS_OPTYPES] selecting which buffer writers
   currently use. The evaluator atomically swaps the index (cmpxchg)
   BEFORE reading/resetting the old buffer, so new writers stop
   landing there from that instant. One narrow residual race remains
   (a writer that already read the old index microseconds before the
   swap can still land one sample there) -- accepted as a
   single-sample-per-window skew at most, the standard tradeoff for
   lock-free double-buffered kernel stats short of a full seqlock.

2. Changing lm_block_size_threshold at runtime previously left
   small_count/large_count history classified under the OLD
   threshold, silently corrupting confidence-gate and prediction
   meaning under the new one. lm_block_size_threshold now has its own
   dedicated setter (split out of the shared LM_CONST_ATTR_RW macro,
   which still generates the other 3 LM constants -- outlier_percentile/
   interval_threshold_ms/samples_threshold don't have this problem,
   since they only affect how FUTURE recalculations behave, not which
   bucket past samples already landed in) that resets each optype's
   model via the same sideload_latency_model(model, 0, 0) the native
   reset_lat_model sysfs already uses, but only when the value
   actually changes.

3. force_shrink_model() moved from the timer callback (softirq
   context) to a proper workqueue (shrink_work, real process context).
   Kept GFP_ATOMIC deliberately rather than switching to GFP_KERNEL --
   that would additionally require restructuring force_shrink_model to
   allocate before taking model->update_lock (a spinlock can't be held
   across a sleeping allocation), a bigger locking change for marginal
   benefit given a failed GFP_ATOMIC shrink already degrades
   gracefully (just skips that attempt). upstream ADIOS's own
   latency_model_update() (unrelated to this patch series) still runs
   from the timer as before -- not touched, not flagged as a problem,
   pre-existing upstream design.

4. last_adjust_jiffies (plain unsigned long, read/written across
   completion-path calls to adapt_depth_controller) now uses READ_ONCE/
   WRITE_ONCE consistently -- was missed in the previous revision's
   READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE pass, which only covered the sysfs-tunable
   fields.

5. lm_adapt_cooldown_ms now READ_ONCE'd at its use site in
   adapt_depth_controller (the setter already used WRITE_ONCE via the
   shared macro from the previous revision; the read site was missed).

6. lm_confidence_samples minimum raised 0 -> 8. At 0, model_is_confident()'s
   check ("count >= 0") was always true, silently disabling the
   confidence gate entirely. Flagged in BOTH review rounds; missed in
   the first round's fix pass.

ALREADY RESOLVED (review's point still listed it, but the previous
revision's fix already covers it): "confidence should be per-bucket,
not just small_count" -- model_is_confident() already takes block_size
and checks small_count vs large_count depending on which bucket the
request actually belongs to, exactly as requested. No new change
needed.

ADVISORY / NOT ACTIONABLE AS CODE CHANGES: interactive-boost false-
positive tuning (idle threshold, boost duration, per-TGID frequency
limits) is genuine tuning guidance, not a bug -- no default change
without real benchmark evidence pointing at a specific number.
Eviction policy for the 32-slot table (oldest-first) is already what's
implemented; the review agrees it should stay at 32 without further
evidence for more. read_priority=-3, batch defaults, and
global_latency_window heuristic status quo are all unchanged --
lowering any of them now would contradict this session's actual
5-round benchmark data without new evidence prompting it.

STILL DEFERRED (unchanged from previous revision, still low
priority): struct reorganization for maintainability (zero behavioral
effect). "Require 2 consistent windows before adjusting" (an
additional layer on top of the cooldown already added) -- reasonable
but the cooldown mechanism already added last revision covers the
same practical goal (rate-limiting oscillation); adding a second,
overlapping mechanism without evidence the first is insufficient adds
complexity for unproven benefit. nr_requests-as-queue-depth-proxy
wording: still heuristic and documented as such; no concretely better
alternative signal was identified as reliably accessible in this exact
kernel backport.

NOT submitted upstream. NOT compiled/tested against a real
android14-6.1 tree yet -- verified only for brace/paren balance,
correct include (workqueue.h added), and correct forward-reference
ordering (force_shrink_model is defined before shrink_work_fn uses
it).
---
